target/arm: evaluate the SPTM pointer-authentication bypass without helpers.

The SPTM base engine never enforces pointer authentication
(QEMU_SPTM_DISABLE_PAC, pinned by tests/python/test_engine_pac.py): PAC*
returns its operand, AUT* returns it with the PAC field refilled from
bit 55 and never fails, XPAC* strips. No PAC algorithm runs for those: the
early returns make pauth_computepac dead in every helper but PACGA (the
built engine has no call to it), and -cpu max on this QEMU defaults to
pauth-impdef (xxhash) anyway, not QARMA5. What every PACIBSP/RETAB,
PACIASP/AUTIASP, BLRAA, LDRAA, XPACI ... still paid was a TCG helper call
plus arm_mmu_idx_el, pauth_check_trap, arm_mmu_idx and aa64_va_parameters
per instruction: on the virt machine (-cpu max, tests/engine/pac-bench.S,
a recent engine) a frame-only call costs 8-9.5 ns and the same call
signed with pacibsp/retab 31-32 ns; PACDZA+AUTDZA+XPACI cost ~14 ns each.

On a CPU without EL2 and EL3 (the vphone board removes both) the trap check
can never fire, so the bypass is a function of the pointer, its bit 55 and
the current translation regime alone:

- PAC* (PACIA/IB/DA/DB, the Z and 1716 forms, PACIASP/PACIBSP) emit nothing;
- AUT*, the combined RETA*/BRA*/BLRA*/ERETA*, LDRAA/LDRAB and XPAC* become
  an inline strip: dst = bit55 ? src | mask : src & ~mask, with mask read
  from env->tomacco_pauth_strip[row][bit55];
- rebuild_hflags_a64 records those masks whenever it sets PAUTH_ACTIVE:
  pauth_ptr_mask(aa64_va_parameters()) of the stage 1 regime per half,
  instruction and data, and 0 for AUT* with a key arm_sctlr() leaves off.
  Every change of an input (TCR, SCTLR, EL, HCR) already rebuilds the hflags
  the translated code was built for.

PACGA keeps its helper. The tomacco-pauth-fast CPU property (default on)
restores the helper path, so one binary can be compared with itself
(-global max-arm-cpu.tomacco-pauth-fast=off). The results are those of the
helpers for every TCR/SCTLR shape tests/engine/pac-sweep.S runs (checked
against a model of the helpers, the previous engine and the helper path).

diff --git a/target/arm/internals.h b/target/arm/internals.h
--- a/target/arm/internals.h
+++ b/target/arm/internals.h
@@ -1886,6 +1886,50 @@
     return mask;
 }
 
+/*
+ * The SPTM base engine does not enforce pointer authentication: PAC* returns
+ * its operand, AUT* returns it with the PAC field refilled from bit 55 and
+ * never fails, XPAC* strips as architected and PACGA computes a code
+ * (target/arm/tcg/pauth_helper.c; tests/python/test_engine_pac.py pins it).
+ * Apple's PAC implementation does weird things after launchd starts and there
+ * are multiple processes; use this to straight up disable PAC for now.
+ */
+#define QEMU_SPTM_DISABLE_PAC
+
+/*
+ * Rows of CPUARMState.tomacco_pauth_strip: the mask AUT* with that key, or
+ * XPACI/XPACD, removes from a pointer in the current translation regime.
+ */
+enum {
+    TOMACCO_PAUTH_IA,
+    TOMACCO_PAUTH_IB,
+    TOMACCO_PAUTH_DA,
+    TOMACCO_PAUTH_DB,
+    TOMACCO_PAUTH_XPACI,
+    TOMACCO_PAUTH_XPACD,
+    TOMACCO_PAUTH_ROWS,
+};
+
+/*
+ * tomacco_pauth_fast:
+ * Whether this CPU evaluates the bypass without the pauth helpers: PAC* as
+ * a no-op and AUT* and XPAC* as an inline strip with the masks
+ * rebuild_hflags_a64 records (PACGA keeps its helper). That is exact
+ * only when pauth_check_trap() can never trap, i.e. without EL2 and EL3
+ * (HCR_EL2.API, SCR_EL3.API). The tomacco-pauth-fast CPU property (default
+ * on) turns it off for comparison.
+ */
+static inline bool tomacco_pauth_fast(CPUARMState *env)
+{
+#ifdef QEMU_SPTM_DISABLE_PAC
+    return env_archcpu(env)->tomacco_pauth_fast &&
+           !arm_feature(env, ARM_FEATURE_EL2) &&
+           !arm_feature(env, ARM_FEATURE_EL3);
+#else
+    return false;
+#endif
+}
+
 /* Add the cpreg definitions for debug related system registers */
 void define_debug_regs(ARMCPU *cpu);
 
